= Cell membrane =

== Overview ==
A '''cell membrane''' is a lipid-and-protein membrane that defines a cell boundary and mediates exchange and communication between the cell and its environment.

== Definition ==
The plasma membrane is built around a phospholipid bilayer with associated proteins. It separates the cytoplasm from the external environment while membrane proteins perform functions such as selective transport, signaling, adhesion, and enzymatic activity.

== Scope & importance ==
The membrane makes cellular organization possible by maintaining different conditions inside and outside the cell while permitting controlled interaction across the boundary.

== Core concepts ==
Core features include the phospholipid bilayer, integral and peripheral membrane proteins, selective permeability, membrane transport, receptors, and dynamic interactions with the cytoskeleton and extracellular environment.

== Examples ==
Ion channels allow selected ions to cross membranes. Receptors bind extracellular signals and trigger intracellular responses. Transport proteins move specific molecules that cannot freely cross the lipid bilayer.

== Applications / implications ==
Membrane biology is central to physiology, neuroscience, pharmacology, microbiology, biotechnology, and the study of cellular energetics and signaling.

== Epistemic notes ==
The 'fluid mosaic' model remains a useful organizing model, but real membranes are heterogeneous, dynamic structures whose composition and organization vary among cells and organelles.

